{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And HaShem relented.</b> Regret comes about for three reasons: failure of the one who vows, inability of the one who vows, or a change of mind. Two of them apply to a person — namely inability of the one who vows or a change of mind. The third is stated in many places regarding the Holy One, blessed be He — namely failure of the one vowed upon. For example here, and for example 'I have regretted that I made Saul king' (1 Samuel 15:11), and in many places regarding the Holy One, blessed be He; and so it is written, 'At one moment I may speak concerning a nation to uproot and to tear down, and that nation turns back from its evil, and I relent concerning the evil I intended to do to it; and at one moment I may speak concerning a nation to build and to plant, and it does evil in My eyes, and I relent concerning the good' (Jeremiah 18:7–10). And what is written 'and the son of man, that You should be grieved' (Psalms 144:3?) — that refers to non-sinners, as it is written 'He does not look at iniquity in Jacob' (Numbers 23:21), and then there is no occasion for regret or doing harm." "<b>To His heart.</b> That He took counsel to create it (the expression ויתעצב read as related to עצה, counsel). Another interpretation: <b>to His heart.</b> It refers to man — the Holy One, blessed be He, was grieved on account of the heart of man, which was thinking evil thoughts, like 'for they were grieved on account of David' (1 Samuel 22:22?), which means on account of David. Another interpretation: <b>to His heart.</b> עציבה means nothing but mourning, as it is said 'for the king was grieved for his son' (2 Samuel 19:3) — meaning He mourned over His world. And if you ask: does one mourn before the deceased has died? A person, who does not know the moments of time, cannot mourn until the deceased dies; but the Holy One, blessed be He, for whom everything is revealed and known, and who lives forever, mourns beforehand. Another interpretation: <b>to His heart.</b> Man was [grieved] to the heart of the Holy One, blessed be He — like an annoying and despicable thing in the eyes of the Holy One, blessed be He."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The phrase 'ובן אדם ויתנחם' does not correspond to a standard verse; translated hyper-literally as Chizkuni cites it. Similarly 'פשיעת הנידר' (failure of the one vowed upon) appears alongside 'פשיעת הנודר' (failure of the one who vows); both rendered faithfully as found.", 1 "The first interpretation reads ויתעצב as related to עצה (counsel); rendered literally as Chizkuni implies. The citation 'כי נעצב אל דוד' does not match a standard verse exactly; translated hyper-literally. The bracketed [נעצב] follows the supplied text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:03:17.657432578Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}